ancial Hub"}, {"name": "Amsterdam, Netherlands", "role": "Early Banking Center"}, {"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Global Financial Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: bank secrecy", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bank-secrecy"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q806774", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank_secrecy"], "tags": ["statute", "Ancient Legal Codes (Hammurabi, Roman)", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Key lasting impacts include: Shaped diplomatic relations and international order. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Bank secrecy has significantly influenced global finance, enabling the establishment of tax havens and shaping international economic policies, with profound implications for government regulations.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-29T05:37:35Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30425477872", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The agreement between a financial institution and its client", "newValue": "Bank secrecy, a critical financial doctrine, emerged prominently in the late 17th century, particularly in jurisdictions such as Switzerland and the Netherlands, where laws were enacted to protect the confidentiality of client information held by financial institutions. Its significance lies in the establishment of trust in banking systems, which facilitated economic growth and attracted foreign investment, shaping modern banking practices globally. Prominent figures like Johann Heinrich von Thurn und Taxis were instrumental in developing postal banking systems that contributed to the broader acceptance of bank secrecy, while cities like Geneva became known as havens for financial privacy.

The enactment of stringent privacy laws, particularly in Switzerland with the Banking Law of 1934, solidified bank secrecy as a cornerstone of the financial industry. This law prohibited banks from disclosing the identities of account holders, enabling individuals and corporations to store their wealth away from governmental scrutiny. The phenomenon accelerated during the 20th century, as global conflicts and economic crises drove wealthy clients to seek the security offered by confidential banking environments, notably during the rise of post-war tax havens and offshore banking.

